course: dictionary
lesson: 2026
title: Confidently: sure of yourself
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Confidently is an adverb. It tells you how someone does something, and it means in a way that shows you believe in yourself and are not afraid. «Give it a brand-new riddle, a silly trick question or a big sum, and it may answer just as confidently and get it badly wrong.» The clue is the word answer: the reply is said with a sure, bold voice, but that does not mean the reply is right.
explain: Confidently can also mean while feeling sure that something is true or will happen. A teacher might say a pupil «speaks confidently» because the pupil is calm and sure. A reader might «reads confidently» when they are not stopping to doubt each word. In a story, «confidently» shows a brave, sure character who knows what to do.
explain: Use the swap check. Put a word like certain or sure in place of confidently, and see if the sentence still works. «She walked confidently onto the stage» can become «She walked certainly onto the stage», which is a bit odd, but «She walked sure of herself onto the stage» works well. Notice the feeling in the word, and you will spot it more easily next time.
fact: Confidently is an adverb, and it describes how something is done.
fact: The comparative is more confidently, and the superlative is most confidently.
fact: «Confidently» means in a way that shows belief in yourself and no fear.
fact: «Confidently» can also mean feeling sure that something is true or will happen, as in «confidently predict».
fact: Being confident is about how you feel and how you act, so a confident answer is not always a correct answer.
exercise: «Give it a brand-new riddle, a silly trick question or a big sum, and it may answer just as confidently and get it badly wrong.» What does confidently mean here? | Afraid and shaking | Slowly and quietly, like a whisper | *Sure of itself, with no doubt in its voice | Sorry, as if it knew it was wrong
exercise: Mia reads the poem «confidently» in front of the class. What does this tell us about Mia? | She is scared and reads very fast | *She is not afraid, and she believes in herself | She reads with her eyes closed | She has never seen the poem before
exercise: «She walked confidently onto the stage.» Find and copy the adverb that describes how she walked. | onto | stage | the | *confidently
test: The Otter says, «you can confidently rely upon me». What does confidently suggest? | Quietly, as if in a whisper | Angry and unkind | Sleepy and slow | *Certain and trustworthy, with no doubt
test: Sam confidently predicted the winner of the race. Which word is closest in meaning to confidently here? | Angrily | Hesitantly | Sleepily | *Certainly
exam: Can someone answer confidently and still be wrong? | No, a confident answer is always correct | *Yes, because confidence shows how sure you feel, not that you are right | Yes, but only in made-up stories | No, because confidently is a noun
